   "html_text": "The model of a space of possible outcomes of a throw of a die gives us an initial idea of what the distinctive properties of state of affairs are and why it is plausible to assume that there are any states of affairs. It leaves questions about the precise nature of states of affairs open. For example, Wittgenstein (1918: 4.26) argued that only elementary sentences “picture” states of affairs. An elementary sentence in Wittgenstein’s sense is a concatenation of simple proper names each of which r",
